"tokens_after": 43604, "ok": true, "error": ""}], "final_claim": {"task_id": "t_cd75e8ee", "decision": "present", "mechanism": "regime_switch", "entities": [103, 462, 719, 836, 866, 1224, 1318, 1374, 1415, 1598, 1610, 1826], "window": [35166, 45166], "confidence": 0.82, "narrative": "The group undergoes an abrupt, coordinated regime switch at t\u224840166. With \u00b15000 half-windows, every member tested (103, 462, 836, 1224, 1415, 1598, 1826) has exactly zero overlap between its pre- and post-contact sets (Jaccard 0.00), and the sets are internally stable: node 103 reuses the same partners across three pre-windows and then a different stable pool across three post-windows, so this is two regimes joined by a sharp boundary rather than gradual drift. The switch is collective \u2014 all members drop a common old cohort {57,234,235,354,535,559,641,931,1010,1141,1182,1357,1416,1436,1838} and adopt a common new cohort {156,255,324,402,617,728,756,874,1133,1312,1422,1538,1656,1779,1893} simultaneously, and the two cohorts are temporally disjoint. Confounders were checked: group event volume is flat/dipping (the transition falls in a low-volume bin), members are non-hubs with uniform degree, and while control hubs 733/1566 also show partner churn, their turnover is idiosyncratic and unsynchronised, unlike the group's shared-cohort, single-instant replacement. Confidence held below 0.9 because ambient churn in this graph is high and node 719 was too sparse near the boundary to confirm individually.", "evidence": [{"trace_id": "step_04", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_05", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_06", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_07", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_08", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_09", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_10", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_11", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_12", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_13", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_14", "rol